{\sffamily\bfseries\color{white}\large ECON 803 \textbar{} MICROECONOMIC THEORY I\par}
\vspace{0.47in}
{\sffamily\bfseries\color{white}\fontsize{32}{35}\selectfont
The Economic KKT\\Cookbook\par}
\vspace{0.18in}
{\sffamily\color{white}\fontsize{15}{19}\selectfont
A portable method for constrained optimization, corners,\\
shadow prices, and comparative statics\par}

\vspace{1.12in}
\begin{tcolorbox}[enhanced,boxrule=0pt,arc=2pt,colback=KKTbluewash,
  left=14pt,right=14pt,top=12pt,bottom=12pt]
{\sffamily\bfseries\large\color{KKTnavy} What this booklet gives you}\par
\vspace{3pt}
One sign convention. One active-set algorithm. One verification checklist.
The method applies to consumer choice, firms, portfolios, mechanism constraints,
and dynamic problems solved one state at a time. A separate algebraic toolkit
shows when structure can solve most of the problem before the multipliers appear.

\vspace{5pt}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{p{0.18\textwidth}X}
\textbf{\color{KKTnavy}Notation} & Write every inequality as a nonnegative slack before assigning a multiplier.\\
\textbf{\color{KKTnavy}Corners} & Replace unjustified equalities by complementarity and sign checks.\\
\textbf{\color{KKTnavy}Shortcuts} & Use tangent directions, bounds, homogeneity, duality, and projection before brute force.\\
\textbf{\color{KKTnavy}Interpretation} & Read multipliers as marginal values only after the candidate is verified.\\
\textbf{\color{KKTnavy}Examples} & Expected-utility portfolio choice and a Galor-inspired quantity-quality model.\\
\end{tabularx}
\end{tcolorbox}

\vfill
{\sffamily\bfseries\large\color{KKTnavy} Victor H. Aguiar}\par
{\sffamily\color{KKTgray} Simon Fraser University \textbar{} Fall 2026}\par
\vspace{0.24in}
{\small\color{KKTgray}
Student edition. The examples are fully worked so that the booklet can be used
for review, office hours, and core-exam preparation.}%
}

