\begin_layout Section
Eliminate multipliers without losing constraints
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose the active equalities are 
\begin_inset Formula $Bx=d$
\end_inset

. A feasible first-order movement 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 along that face satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $Bz=0$
\end_inset

. At an interior point of the face, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\boxed{z^{\top}\nabla f(x^*)=0\quad\text{for every }z\in\ker B.}
\]
\end_inset

If the columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 span 
\begin_inset Formula $\ker B$
\end_inset

, solve 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{\top}\nabla f(x)=0$
\end_inset

 together with 
\begin_inset Formula $Bx=d$
\end_inset

. This is the matrix version of substituting the budget and often removes all equality multipliers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With one budget 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=m$
\end_inset

, a budget-neutral movement between positive goods 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p_k f_i-p_i f_k=0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\frac{f_i}{p_i}=\frac{f_k}{p_k}.
\]
\end_inset

The familiar marginal-utility-per-dollar rule is therefore a tangent-direction condition. It applies only after positivity has been established.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Change from assets to state goods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the portfolio example, let 
\begin_inset Formula $c=(c_L,c_H)^{\top}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \[
c_L=\tfrac12\alpha+\beta,
\qquad
c_H=\tfrac74\alpha+\beta.
\]
\end_inset

Inverting this payoff map gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\alpha=\frac45(c_H-c_L),
\qquad
\beta=\frac15(7c_L-2c_H).
\]
\end_inset

Consequently, the asset budget becomes a state-price budget: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\frac35c_L+\frac25c_H\le w.}
\label{eq:state-price-budget}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

No short selling survives as the consumption cone 
\begin_inset Formula \[
c_H\ge c_L,
\qquad
2c_H\le7c_L.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

