\begin_layout Section
Cauchy–Schwarz solves square-root utility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider state prices 
\begin_inset Formula $q_s>0$
\end_inset

, probabilities 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_s>0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_s q_sc_s\le w$
\end_inset

. Then 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_s\pi_s\sqrt{c_s}
=\sum_s\frac{\pi_s}{\sqrt{q_s}}\sqrt{q_sc_s}
\le
\sqrt{\sum_s\frac{\pi_s^2}{q_s}}
\sqrt{\sum_s q_sc_s}.
\]
\end_inset

For the portfolio state prices in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:state-price-budget"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{(1/2)^2}{3/5}+\frac{(1/2)^2}{2/5}=\frac{25}{24}.
\]
\end_inset

Thus, at 
\begin_inset Formula $w=24$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
F(c_L,c_H)\le\sqrt{\frac{25}{24}\,24}=5.
\]
\end_inset

Equality in Cauchy–Schwarz requires 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sqrt{c_s}\propto\frac{\pi_s}{q_s},
\qquad
\sqrt{c_L}:\sqrt{c_H}=2:3.
\]
\end_inset

The state-price budget then gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
(c_L,c_H)=(16,36),
\qquad
(\alpha,\beta)=(16,8).
\]
\end_inset

The holding-cone inequalities hold, so this sharp upper bound is attainable. It proves global optimality before KKT is written.
