\begin_layout Section
Dot the KKT system with the allocation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $Ax\le b$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\ge0$
\end_inset

, complementarity implies 
\begin_inset Formula \[
x^{\top}\nabla f(x)
=x^{\top}A^{\top}\lambda
=\lambda^{\top}Ax
=\lambda^{\top}b.
\]
\end_inset

Hence the compact KKT system contains the useful identity 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{x^{\top}\nabla f(x)=\lambda^{\top}b.}
\label{eq:euler-kkt}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

, Euler's theorem turns 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:euler-kkt"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 into 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\boxed{r f(x^*)=\lambda^{*\top}b.}
\]
\end_inset

With one binding budget 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x\le m$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\boxed{\lambda^*=\frac{rV(m)}{m}.}
\]
\end_inset

This agrees with the envelope theorem because scaling income by 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 scales the value by 
\begin_inset Formula $t^r$
\end_inset

 whenever the feasible set scales linearly.
\end_layout

