 positive definite, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
f(x)=c+a^{\top}x-\frac12x^{\top}Qx
=C-\frac12(x-\bar x)^{\top}Q(x-\bar x),
\qquad
\bar x=Q^{-1}a.
\]
\end_inset

Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar x$
\end_inset

 is the unconstrained bliss point, and over a closed convex feasible set the constrained optimizer is its projection in the metric generated by 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

. Active constraints are the faces hit by that projection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the reduced quantity-quality objective, put 
\begin_inset Formula \[
a=6-p,
\qquad b=4-k.
\]
\end_inset

Then 
\begin_inset Formula \[
W(n,H)
=y+\frac12a^2+\frac14b^2
-\frac12\left[(n-a)^2+2\left(H-\frac b2\right)^2\right].
\]
\end_inset

The bliss point is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\boxed{(n_0,H_0)=\left(6-p,\frac{4-k}{2}\right).}
\]
\end_inset

The three demographic snapshots are different weighted projections of this point onto the subsistence set and quality cone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Once a face is guessed, solve one linear system
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let the proposed active constraints be 
\begin_inset Formula $Bx=d$
\end_inset

. Stationarity and feasibility are 
\begin_inset Formula \[
Qx+B^{\top}\lambda=a,
\qquad Bx=d,
\]
\end_inset

or 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{
\begin{pmatrix}Q&B^{\top}\\ B&0\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}x\\ \lambda\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}a\\ d\end{pmatrix}.}
\label{eq:kkt-matrix}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $BQ^{-1}B^{\top}$
\end_inset

 is invertible, the Schur complement gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda=(BQ^{-1}B^{\top})^{-1}(BQ^{-1}a-d).
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

