\begin_layout Section
Turn a cone into a one-dimensional problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the quantity-quality model, when 
\begin_inset Formula $n>0$
\end_inset

 define 
\begin_inset Formula \[
q=\frac{H}{n}\in[s,4].
\]
\end_inset

Using 
\begin_inset Formula $a=6-p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b=4-k$
\end_inset

, the gain relative to the apex becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \[
W(n,qn)-W(0,0)
=(a+bq)n-\left(\frac12+q^2\right)n^2.
\]
\end_inset

Conditional on the ray 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

, the unconstrained choice is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{n_0(q)=\frac{[a+bq]_+}{1+2q^2}.}
\label{eq:ray-choice}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

The subsistence constraint truncates this at 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\max}(q)$
\end_inset

. For 
\begin_inset Formula $q>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
n_{\max}(q)=
\frac{-(p+kq)+
\sqrt{(p+kq)^2+2q^2(y-1)}}{q^2},
\]
\end_inset

while 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\max}(0)=(y-1)/p$
\end_inset

. Hence a two-choice nonlinear problem reduces to a bounded search over 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in[s,4]$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula \[
n^*(q)=\min\{n_0(q),n_{\max}(q)\}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

