\begin_layout Subsection*
Step 5: audit the candidate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At 
\begin_inset Formula $(c_L,c_H)=(16,36)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
F_{\alpha}=\frac1{32}+\frac7{96}=\frac5{48},
\;
F_{\beta}=\frac1{16}+\frac1{24}=\frac5{48}.
\]
\end_inset

Both asset holdings and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 are positive, the budget slack is zero, and both stationarity residuals are zero. All complementary products vanish. The payoff vectors are linearly independent, so strict concavity of square-root utility makes 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 strictly concave in 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha,\beta)$
\end_inset

 on the relevant domain. The verified KKT point is therefore the unique global maximizer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Step 6: read the economics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because a one-dollar shift from the safe asset to the risky asset changes the state payoff by 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde R-1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
F_{\alpha}-F_{\beta}
=\E\!\left[u'(c)(\widetilde R-1)\right].
\]
\end_inset

Thus the familiar expected-marginal-utility rule is exactly the interior KKT condition. The envelope theorem gives 
\begin_inset Formula $V'(24)=\lambda^*=5/48$
\end_inset

: one more dollar of initial wealth raises maximal expected utility by approximately 
\begin_inset Formula $0.1042$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

