\begin_layout Section
Why KKT is safer than an interior heuristic
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose instead that the consumer is risk neutral, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c)=c$
\end_inset

. The risky asset's expected gross return is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\E[\widetilde R]
=\frac12\left(\frac12+\frac74\right)=\frac98>1.
\]
\end_inset

The optimum is the corner 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha^*,\beta^*)=(24,0)$
\end_inset

. Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^*=9/8$
\end_inset

. Then 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda^*-F_{\alpha}=0,
\;
\lambda^*-F_{\beta}=\frac18>0.
\]
\end_inset

The second inequality is the correct condition at the zero safe holding. Forcing equality would incorrectly rule out the optimum.
\end_layout

